1
00:05:41,538 --> 00:05:47,538
Spectre is a action, adventure, thriller film directed by Sam Mendes.

2
00:11:23,076 --> 00:11:29,076
A cryptic message from Bond’s past sends him on a
trail to uncover a sinister organization.

3
00:17:04,615 --> 00:17:10,615
While M battles political forces to keep the secret service alive, Bond peels back
the layers of deceit to reveal the terrible truth behind SPECTRE.

4
00:22:46,153 --> 00:22:52,153
James Bond, played by Daniel Craig.

5
00:28:27,692 --> 00:28:33,692
Blofeld, played by Christoph Waltz.

6
00:34:09,230 --> 00:34:15,230
Madeleine, played by Léa Seydoux.

7
00:39:50,769 --> 00:39:56,769
M, played by Ralph Fiennes.

8
00:45:32,307 --> 00:45:38,307
Lucia, played by Monica Bellucci.

9
00:51:13,846 --> 00:51:19,846
Q, played by Ben Whishaw.

10
00:56:55,384 --> 00:57:01,384
Spectre is a 2015 spy thriller film directed by Sam Mendes and written by John Logan, Neal Purvis,
Robert Wade, and Jez Butterworth from a story conceived by Logan, Purvis, and Wade.

11
01:02:36,923 --> 01:02:42,923
The sequel to Skyfall (2012), it is the twenty-fourth installment in the James Bond film series produced by
Eon Productions and the fourth film to star Daniel Craig as the fictional MI6 agent James Bond.

12
01:08:18,461 --> 01:08:24,461
The film co-stars Christoph Waltz, Léa Seydoux, Ben Whishaw, Naomie
Harris, Dave Bautista, Monica Bellucci, and Ralph Fiennes.

13
01:14:00,000 --> 01:14:06,000
It was the last Bond film to be co-distributed by Sony Pictures Releasing.

14
01:19:41,538 --> 01:19:47,538
In the film, Bond battles Spectre, an international crime
organisation led by Ernst Stavro Blofeld (Waltz).

15
01:25:23,076 --> 01:25:29,076
Despite initially stating he would not direct Spectre, Mendes confirmed his return in 2014 after Nicolas Winding Refn
declined to direct; Mendes became the first to direct successive James Bond films since John Glen.

16
01:31:04,615 --> 01:31:10,615
The inclusion of Spectre and its associated characters marked the end of the Thunderball controversy, in which Kevin McClory and Fleming were embroiled in
lengthy legal disputes over the film rights to the novel; Spectre is the first film to feature these elements since Diamonds Are Forever (1971).

17
01:36:46,153 --> 01:36:52,153
Following the Sony Pictures hack, it was revealed Sony and Eon clashed regarding finance, stunts, and
filming locations; Spectre is estimated to have a final budget of $245–300 million.

18
01:42:27,692 --> 01:42:33,692
Principal photography began in December 2014 and lasted until July 2015, with filming
locations including Austria, the United Kingdom, Italy, Morocco, and Mexico.

19
01:48:09,230 --> 01:48:15,230
Spectre premiered at the Royal Albert Hall on 26 October 2015 and was theatrically released in conventional
and IMAX formats in the United Kingdom that day, and in the United States on 6 November.

20
01:53:50,769 --> 01:53:56,769
The film received mixed reviews from critics, who praised the action sequences, cast performances (particularly Craig's
and Bautista's), and the musical score, but criticised the pacing and formulaic narrative decisions.

21
01:59:32,307 --> 01:59:38,307
It grossed $880 million worldwide, making it the sixth-highest-grossing film of 2015 and the second-highest
grossing James Bond film after Skyfall, unadjusted for inflation (fourth when adjusted).

22
02:05:13,846 --> 02:05:19,846
The film's theme song, "Writing's on the Wall", won an Academy
Award and Golden Globe for Best Original Song.

23
02:10:55,384 --> 02:11:01,384
The next film in the series, No Time to Die, was released in 2021.

24
02:16:36,923 --> 02:16:42,923
The film explores themes of based on novel or
book, spy, secret agent, sequel, mi6.

25
02:22:18,461 --> 02:22:24,461
The tone is intense.
